---
// Foundation, edit with care
// Site footer, in the church's own grammar (Plan 2a task 9; redrawn by the
// art-direction pass, plan 3 task 5).
//
// It is ONE DARK BAND on the indigo field, in both themes. That is deliberate,
// not an oversight: the footer is the closing surface of every page, and the
// church identity ends a page the way it opens one, on indigo. The band is
// --color-indigo-deep, the deeper of the two indigos, so gold labels and taupe
// body copy clear AA on it (both pairs are asserted in theme-tokens.test.ts).
//
// Because the band is fixed-dark, the ink on it is text-bg, never text-cream:
// --color-cream FLIPS to ink under .dark (see globals.css) while --color-bg
// stays paper-white in both themes. Getting that wrong gives you dark text on
// a dark band in one theme only, which is the kind of bug nobody sees until it
// ships. Same reason the wordmark here is the DARK-MODE logo file in both
// themes and carries no data-theme-logo attribute: there is nothing to swap.
//
// Gold is used on the indigo ground only, where it clears AA: the poster
// eyebrow, the column labels, the goals' glyphs and the watchword mark. Gold
// on cream is 2.37:1 and is never used for text anywhere in this build.
//
// Structure (top to bottom), redrawn 2026-09-24 in the church identity (the
// footer identity pass; rollout plan: "footer carries the Praise & Proclaim
// mark and the four goals"):
//   1. Optional newsletter signup (only when siteSettings.newsletter.enabled)
//   2. THE POSTER ROW, the closing Sunday band: the live dated line, the
//      service time at poster scale, the street line as the lede, and the gold
//      Give button. The same promise the hero opens with, which is what makes
//      the header and the footer read as one object. Because it gives the time,
//      the street and Give on every page, nothing below repeats them.
//   3. THE FOUR GOALS (GoalsRow): each goal's building glyph in gold, its name
//      and its small line, linking to its band on Who We Are.
//   4. Three columns on one left edge, sharing the max-w-content px-gutter
//      container the header and every church block use:
//        a. Pages, the editor's first footer column, set in two short
//           columns. No numbers: a page list is not a sequence (rollout plan
//           rule 11), so it is a <ul>.
//        b. Elsewhere, the editor's second column, the same way.
//        c. Office: the office hours, the phone, the email. The street
//           address only when the poster row above did not print it. Then
//           the church's accounts elsewhere as round icon buttons
//           (socialLinksOf: Facebook, Instagram, YouTube).
//   5. THE SIGN-OFF: the Praise & Proclaim mark (WatchwordMark) and the bottom
//      line, "An American Baptist congregation in downtown Muncie since
//      1859", over the Hannaford rendering drawn as faint gold line art along
//      the bottom edge.
//   6. Thin base rail: the wordmark (the footer's link home), auto-year
//      copyright, small print (the theme control came off 2026-09-24: light-only)
//
// THE RENDERING. Samuel Hannaford and Sons' 1927 drawing of the building (the
// same drawing Home's "Our Building" band shows), baked into the repo as
// src/assets/footer-rendering.webp: the pencil lines lifted off the paper
// (background divided out, levels, four alpha steps, edges faded), 952x452,
// lossless WebP with alpha, ~31 KB. It is not an <img>: it is a CSS MASK on a
// gold-filled box, so the drawing takes the brand gold token itself (no
// colour in the file, no hex in the code) at low opacity. It is absolutely
// positioned and aria-hidden, so it moves no layout and is never a candidate
// for the page's largest paint.
//
// Tailwind v4 gotcha: max-w-xs/s/m/l are shadowed by this project's custom
// --spacing-* tokens (they resolve to a few px, not the container scale). Use
// max-w-md / max-w-2xl / arbitrary values like max-w-[28rem] for text widths.

import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import { site } from '@/data/site';
import { Mail, Phone } from 'lucide-react';
import NewsletterSignup from '@/components/NewsletterSignup';
// THE SVG, NOT A PNG, and the same files the header uses.
//
// Until 2026-09-08 this imported logo-{light,dark}.png, which were a REAL
// CLIENT'S wordmark left in the starter from the build it was forked from.
// Every project made from this starter therefore shipped somebody else's logo
// in its footer, and at least one of them reached a live site that way. The
// header had always used the neutral placeholder SVGs; only the footer was
// wrong, because nobody scrolls to the bottom of a page they are building.
//
// Keep both chrome components on the SAME assets. If they ever diverge again,
// this is the failure mode. The footer picks the DARK-MODE file of the pair in
// both themes, because the band under it is dark in both themes.
import logoDarkAsset from '@/assets/logo-dark.svg';
import renderingAsset from '@/assets/footer-rendering.webp';
import GoalsRow from '@/components/church/GoalsRow.astro';
import WatchwordMark from '@/components/church/WatchwordMark.astro';
import SocialIcon from '@/components/SocialIcon';
import { socialLinksOf, socialLinkLabel } from '@/lib/social-links';
import { resolveSiteSettings, type RawChromeSettings } from '@/lib/siteSettings';
import { telHref } from '@/lib/phone';
import { splitStega, reattachStega } from '@/lib/preview-stega';
import { clockParts, staticSunday, timeOnly } from '@/lib/live-sunday';

// Passed through Astro's pipeline for a cache-busted URL. No width variants and
// no webp conversion: this is an SVG, so one file is sharp at every size and
// asking the optimizer to rasterise it would be a downgrade.
const footerLogo = await getImage({ src: logoDarkAsset });

interface NewsletterConfig {
  enabled?: boolean;
  heading?: string;
  blurb?: string;
  buttonLabel?: string;
  successMessage?: string;
  consentNote?: string;
  formActionUrl?: string | null;
  audienceId?: string | null;
}

/** One portable-text paragraph, as much of it as this footer needs. */
interface PortableTextBlock {
  _key?: string;
  children?: { text?: string | null }[] | null;
}

interface Props {
  siteSettings?:
    | (RawChromeSettings & {
        title?: string;
        email?: string;
        phone?: string;
        address?: string;
        /** "Sundays at 10:45 am". Drives the poster row; absent means no row. */
        serviceTime?: string;
        officeHours?: PortableTextBlock[] | null;
        socialInstagram?: string;
        socialFacebook?: string;
        socialLinks?: { platform?: string; url?: string; label?: string }[] | null;
        youtubeUrl?: string;
        tagline?: string;
        footerCredit?: string;
        footerCreditUrl?: string;
        newsletter?: NewsletterConfig | null;
      })
    | null;
}

const { siteSettings } = Astro.props as Props;

// The chrome (footer columns, the small-print row, the social switch) resolves
// through one shared helper (see src/lib/siteSettings.ts) so the footer can't
// drift from the header.
const chrome = resolveSiteSettings(siteSettings);

const brandName = siteSettings?.title ?? site.name;
const email = siteSettings?.email ?? '';
const phone = siteSettings?.phone ?? '';

// The Office column. Office hours are portable text, one paragraph per line;
// this footer needs the plain text of each, so the spans are joined rather than
// handed to an island. Joining (never SPLITTING) also keeps any stega payload
// in the preview intact.
const officeLines: string[] = (siteSettings?.officeHours ?? [])
  .map((block) =>
    (block?.children ?? [])
      .map((span) => span?.text ?? '')
      .join('')
      .trim(),
  )
  .filter(Boolean);

// The street address is one Site settings field holding one line per line.
// Cleaned through splitStega BEFORE splitting and trimming: the stega payload
// is written in U+200B/C/D and U+FEFF, and trim() eats U+FEFF, so splitting the
// raw string shatters the payload and click-to-edit stops resolving on the
// address. The payload goes back onto the first line, which keeps the field
// editable in the preview. Same rule the officeHours join above states.
const addressSplit = splitStega(
  typeof siteSettings?.address === 'string' ? siteSettings.address : '',
);
const addressLines: string[] = addressSplit.cleaned
  .split('\n')
  .map((line) => line.trim())
  .filter(Boolean)
  .map((line, i) => (i === 0 ? reattachStega(line, addressSplit.encoded) : line));

// The poster row's third line: the whole address on ONE line, because there it
// is a caption under the time rather than a postal block. Joined, never split
// again, so the payload rejoined above survives.
const streetLine = addressLines.join(', ');

// THE LIVE DATED LINE, exactly as the tall hero draws it (src/components/
// Hero.astro). The SERVER renders the dateless form, which is true whenever
// the page is served and true with JavaScript off; data-live-sunday carries
// the bare time for the upgrade script in BaseLayout, which rewrites the text
// from the VISITOR's clock. serviceTime is CLEANED of its stega payload first:
// both functions measure and rewrite the string, and the payload's U+FEFF
// matches `\s` in a regex, so an encoded value would smuggle invisible
// characters into an attribute (CLAUDE.md, the preview rules).
const serviceTimeClean = siteSettings?.serviceTime
  ? splitStega(siteSettings.serviceTime).cleaned.trim()
  : '';
const showPoster = serviceTimeClean.length > 0;
const posterEyebrow = showPoster ? staticSunday(serviceTimeClean) : '';
// The big line is the TIME only: the eyebrow above it already says Sundays,
// and a poster does not say the same word twice.
const posterTime = showPoster ? timeOnly(serviceTimeClean) : '';
// Castoro Titling has no lower case, so the meridiem is set apart in the
// reading face, as the hymn board sets it: "10:45 am", never "10:45 AM".
const poster = clockParts(posterTime);

// The Give button. The same resolved CTA the header draws, so the two cannot
// disagree about where giving lives or what it is called.
const footerCta = chrome.headerCta;
const footerCtaIsExternal = /^https?:\/\//i.test(footerCta.href);

// Social links: ONE derived list (src/lib/social-links.ts), shared with the
// mobile menu and the Contact page's office door. The stored socialLinks
// array (or the legacy socialInstagram / socialFacebook fields when it is
// empty), plus YouTube derived from youtubeUrl, de-duplicated and ordered
// Facebook, Instagram, YouTube. Gated on the footer's own "show social
// buttons" switch (Site Settings -> Navigation); unset means yes. Each button
// is icon-only, so its accessible name says whose account it is: "First
// Baptist Church Muncie on Facebook", not a bare "Facebook".
const socialLinksToRender = chrome.showFooterSocials ? socialLinksOf(siteSettings) : [];

// Copyright auto-updates every build via new Date(). The credit line is a
// SEPARATE concept that sits next to it in the thin bottom bar.
const currentYear = new Date().getFullYear();

// THE DESIGNER CREDIT (2026-09-24, Nathan). Always shown, the way
// presacademy's colophon does it: Site settings' optional footerCredit /
// footerCreditUrl override it, and the defaults are the studio that built the
// site, so the credit is never missing. The label is drawn in two voices, the
// small UI caps of this rail for "Designed by" and Castoro italic in paper for
// the name, so it is split at its last "by". The split runs on the
// stega-cleaned text (a payload's U+FEFF matches \s) and the payload goes back
// on the name, so click-to-edit still finds the field in the preview. A label
// with no "by" in it is all name.
const creditRaw =
  typeof siteSettings?.footerCredit === 'string' && siteSettings.footerCredit.trim()
    ? siteSettings.footerCredit
    : 'Designed by Nixon Creative Studio';
const creditUrl =
  (typeof siteSettings?.footerCreditUrl === 'string' &&
    splitStega(siteSettings.footerCreditUrl).cleaned.trim()) ||
  'https://www.nixoncreativestudio.com';
const creditSplit = splitStega(creditRaw);
const creditMatch = /^(.*\bby)\s+(\S.*)$/i.exec(creditSplit.cleaned.trim());
const creditPrefix = creditMatch?.[1] ?? '';
const creditName = reattachStega(
  creditMatch?.[2] ?? creditSplit.cleaned.trim(),
  creditSplit.encoded,
);

// Editor-managed footer link columns. Empty is fine: the brand column and the
// Office column below always render, so the footer is never bare.
const sanityFooterColumns = chrome.footerColumns;

// The small-print row at the very bottom. EMPTY means "keep the built-in
// privacy link", which is rendered as its own markup below.
const LEGAL_LINKS = chrome.legalNav;

// One class for every link inside the band, so the four columns cannot drift
// apart. Paper-white at 85%, gold on hover, one focus ring.
const bandLink =
  'text-bg/85 transition-colors hover:text-gold focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-bg focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-transparent rounded-sm';
const columnHeading = 'mb-s font-ui text-[0.75rem] tracking-eyebrow text-gold uppercase';

// The street address prints in the Office column only when the poster row did
// not print it (no service time set, or no address), so a visitor always finds
// it once and never twice.
const showOfficeAddress = !(showPoster && streetLine) && addressLines.length > 0;

// The bottom line. The church's own words: "We Are American Baptists"
// (Beliefs), "Founded in 1859" (History), "the new building in downtown
// Muncie" (History). The year is a fact in site.ts, not a computation.
const bottomLine = `An American Baptist congregation in downtown Muncie since ${site.founded}`;

// The rendering's mask, as a custom property so the scoped <style> below can
// read it. The imported asset's URL is content-hashed by the build.
const renderingStyle = `--footer-rendering: url(${renderingAsset.src}); aspect-ratio: ${renderingAsset.width} / ${renderingAsset.height};`;
---

<footer class="relative isolate overflow-hidden bg-indigo-deep text-bg">
  {
    /* The Hannaford rendering along the bottom edge: gold line art, masked
       from the baked drawing. Decorative, hidden from the tree, and it takes
       no space: absolutely positioned under everything else. It inks in,
       slowly, the first time it scrolls into view (data-reveal="ink",
       2026-09-24; still under prefers-reduced-motion). */
  }
  <div class="footer-rendering" aria-hidden="true" data-reveal="ink" style={renderingStyle}>
    <div class="footer-rendering-ink"></div>
  </div>

  {
    /* Newsletter signup — renders only when siteSettings.newsletter.enabled is
       true. Island hydrates when scrolled into view. */
  }
  {
    siteSettings?.newsletter?.enabled && (
      <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter pt-l">
        <div class="max-w-md">
          <NewsletterSignup client:visible newsletter={siteSettings.newsletter} source="footer" />
        </div>
      </div>
    )
  }

  {
    /* THE POSTER ROW. Not a heading: the page's own h1 already says what the
       page is, and this line is a standing promise rather than a section of
       content, so it is set as type without taking a heading level. */
  }
  {
    showPoster && (
      <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter py-section-md">
        <p class="font-ui text-ui text-gold" data-live-sunday={posterTime}>
          {posterEyebrow}
        </p>
        <p class="mt-[clamp(10px,1.4vh,18px)] font-display text-[clamp(2rem,5.2vw,3.25rem)] leading-none font-normal tracking-[0.01em] uppercase">
          {poster.clock}
          {poster.meridiem && (
            <span class="ml-[.14em] font-body text-[.42em] tracking-normal normal-case italic">
              {poster.meridiem}
            </span>
          )}
        </p>
        {streetLine && (
          <p class="mt-[clamp(12px,1.8vh,20px)] font-body text-lede font-normal text-bg/75 italic">
            {streetLine}
          </p>
        )}
        {footerCta.show && (
          <a
            href={footerCta.href}
            target={footerCtaIsExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
            rel={footerCtaIsExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
            class="press-tactile mt-[clamp(22px,3vh,34px)] inline-flex min-h-[44px] items-center justify-center rounded-sm bg-gold px-[1.6em] py-[1.05em] font-ui text-ui font-semibold text-indigo-deep shadow-[inset_0_0_0_3px_var(--color-gold),inset_0_0_0_4px_var(--color-indigo-deep)] hover:-translate-y-px focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-bg focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-indigo-deep focus-visible:outline-none"
          >
            {footerCta.label}
          </a>
        )}
      </div>
    )
  }

  {/* THE FOUR GOALS, each to its own band on Who We Are. */}
  <div class:list={['relative', showPoster && 'border-t border-bg/15']}>
    <nav aria-label="Our goals" class="mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter py-l">
      <GoalsRow size="footer" />
    </nav>
  </div>

  <div class="relative border-t border-bg/15">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter pt-section-md pb-l">
      <div class="footer-cols grid grid-cols-1 gap-l md:grid-cols-2">
        {
          /* --- a and b. The editor's link columns. A plain list: a page list
             is not a sequence, so it takes no numbers. The first column (the
             long one, Pages) sets its list in two short columns. --- */
        }
        {
          sanityFooterColumns.map((col, colIdx) => (
            <nav
              aria-labelledby={`footer-col-${colIdx}`}
              class:list={[colIdx === 0 && sanityFooterColumns.length > 1 && 'footer-col-wide']}
            >
              <p id={`footer-col-${colIdx}`} class={columnHeading}>
                {col.title}
              </p>
              <ul
                class:list={[
                  'm-0 list-none p-0',
                  colIdx === 0 && col.links.length > 5 && 'footer-list-split',
                ]}
              >
                {col.links.map((link) => (
                  <li class="border-b border-bg/12 py-2">
                    <a href={link.href} class:list={['block font-body text-[1.125rem]', bandLink]}>
                      {link.label}
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </nav>
          ))
        }

        {
          /* --- c. Office: the hours, then how to reach a person. Prose, not a
             link list, so it is drawn here. --- */
        }
        <div>
          <p class={columnHeading}>Office</p>
          <div class="space-y-1 font-body text-bg/80">
            {officeLines.map((line) => <p>{line}</p>)}
          </div>
          <div class="mt-m space-y-1 font-body text-bg/80">
            {
              phone && (
                <p>
                  <a
                    href={telHref(phone)}
                    class:list={['inline-flex items-center gap-xs', bandLink]}
                  >
                    <Phone size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                    {phone}
                  </a>
                </p>
              )
            }
            {
              email && (
                <p class="max-w-full">
                  <a
                    href={`mailto:${email}`}
                    class:list={['inline-flex max-w-full items-center gap-xs break-all', bandLink]}
                  >
                    <Mail size={14} aria-hidden="true" className="shrink-0" />
                    {email}
                  </a>
                </p>
              )
            }
          </div>
          {
            showOfficeAddress && (
              <address class="mt-m space-y-1 font-body text-bg/80 not-italic">
                {addressLines.map((line) => (
                  <p>{line}</p>
                ))}
              </address>
            )
          }
          {
            socialLinksToRender.length > 0 && (
              <ul class="mt-m flex list-none gap-s p-0">
                {socialLinksToRender.map((link) => (
                  <li>
                    <a
                      href={link.url}
                      target="_blank"
                      rel="noopener noreferrer"
                      aria-label={socialLinkLabel(link, brandName)}
                      class="inline-flex h-11 w-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-bg/30 text-bg transition-colors hover:border-gold hover:text-gold focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-gold"
                    >
                      <SocialIcon platform={link.platform} />
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            )
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    /* THE SIGN-OFF: the watchword mark, and the bottom line under it, over
       the rendering. */
  }
  <div class="relative">
    <div class="footer-signoff mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter pt-l pb-section-md">
      <WatchwordMark size="hero" />
      <p class="mt-m max-w-[34ch] font-body text-lede font-normal text-bg/80 italic">
        {bottomLine}
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    /* The base rail — the wordmark, the copyright and the small print,
       on one line so they don't add a tall block.

       The extra `sm:pr-20`: BackToTop is `fixed right-6 bottom-6` and 44px
       wide, so it owns the bottom 68px of the right edge on every page. This
       row turns into a single line at `sm` and puts its links hard against
       that edge, which parked the chip on top of "Privacy policy". 5rem of
       right padding clears the chip with a gap to spare. Below `sm` the same
       5rem sits on the small-print row alone (2026-09-24, the designer
       credit): on a phone the credit is the rail's last line, which is
       exactly where the chip sits, so it wraps short of the chip instead of
       under it, and the copyright line above keeps its full width. */
  }
  <div class="relative border-t border-bg/15">
    <div
      class="mx-auto flex max-w-content flex-col gap-xs px-gutter py-m font-ui text-[0.8125rem] text-bg/70 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:justify-between sm:pr-20"
    >
      <div class="flex items-center gap-m">
        <a
          href="/"
          aria-label={`${brandName} home`}
          class="inline-block shrink-0 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80"
        >
          {
            /* Sized by HEIGHT, with real width/height attributes at the
              wordmark's own 249.45:97.06 aspect (NOT the old 280x60 lockup).
              An unloaded lazy image with width:auto shrink-wraps to 0x0,
              never intersects the viewport and so never loads, which is a
              real bug this family has hit more than once. Give it real
              dimensions. */
          }
          <img
            src={footerLogo.src}
            alt={brandName}
            width={257}
            height={100}
            class="h-10 w-auto"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </a>
        <p>&copy; {currentYear} {brandName}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-m gap-y-xs pr-20 sm:pr-0">
        {
          LEGAL_LINKS.length > 0 ? (
            LEGAL_LINKS.map((l) => (
              <a href={l.href} class:list={['underline underline-offset-2', bandLink]}>
                {l.label}
              </a>
            ))
          ) : (
            <a href="/privacy" class:list={['underline underline-offset-2', bandLink]}>
              Privacy policy
            </a>
          )
        }
        {
          /* The designer credit: quiet, and a little special. The gold rule
            under the name draws in from the left on hover and on keyboard
            focus (.footer-credit below), and appears at once, with no
            draw, for a visitor who asked for less motion. The link is plain
            inline text with a real space between its two voices, so its
            accessible name reads "Designed by Nixon Creative Studio" and it
            wraps at that space on a narrow phone. */
        }
        <a
          href={creditUrl}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="footer-credit rounded-sm focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-bg focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-transparent focus-visible:outline-none"
        >
          {
            creditPrefix && (
              <span class="font-ui text-[0.6875rem] tracking-[0.14em] text-bg/70 uppercase">
                {creditPrefix}
              </span>
            )
          }{' '}
          <span
            class="footer-credit__name ml-1 inline-block font-body text-[0.9375rem] text-bg italic"
          >
            {creditName}
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>

<style>
  /* The credit's gold rule: a pseudo-element scaled from 0 to 1 from its
     left edge, so it draws left to right. Transform only, so nothing reflows.
     Under reduced motion the change is instant (no transition). */
  .footer-credit__name {
    position: relative;
  }
  .footer-credit__name::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: -2px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--color-gold);
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: left center;
  }
  .footer-credit:hover .footer-credit__name::after,
  .footer-credit:focus-visible .footer-credit__name::after {
    transform: scaleX(1);
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .footer-credit__name::after {
      transition: transform 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
    }
  }

  /* Three columns from lg: Pages takes two tracks of five (its list split
     in two short columns), Elsewhere and Office one each. */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .footer-cols {
      grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }
  /* The Pages list in two short columns at every width. */
  .footer-list-split {
    column-count: 2;
    column-gap: var(--spacing-l);
  }
  .footer-list-split > li {
    break-inside: avoid;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1023.98px) {
    .footer-col-wide {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }

  /* The watchword mark, a size between the band and the hero. */
  .footer-signoff :global(.watchword-mark) {
    --mark-a: clamp(52px, 6.2vw, 96px);
    --mark-b: clamp(30px, 4vw, 62px);
  }

  /* The rendering: the brand gold, cut to the drawing's lines by the mask,
